WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:08.460
On the 17th of November, 1961, a young American&nbsp;
man vanished in what was then Dutch New Guinea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.140
After an arduous investigation, the&nbsp;
authorities deemed that he’d drowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:19.200
after his boat capsized a few clicks off the&nbsp;
coast of the town of Agats in the Arafura sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:27.180
His remains were never recovered.&nbsp;
Intriguing? Kind of. But strap yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:33.420
in because two crucial details transform&nbsp;
this tepid tale into a scintillating saga.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.360
First - the man who went missing was&nbsp;
Michael Clark Rockefeller, son of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.760
future vice President and great-grandson&nbsp;
of one of the richest men who ever lived.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.760
And second - contrary to the official report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:55.860
Michael Rockefeller didn't drown in the Autumn&nbsp;
of 1961... Rather, he was eaten by cannibals.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.540
To tell his tale, we must&nbsp;
travel to faraway frontiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.080
unlock the secrets of one of the&nbsp;
planet’s most secluded tribes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:12.180
unravel a governmental coverup that’s lingered for&nbsp;
decades, and confront some unpalatable truths. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.740
and stick around to the end, because&nbsp;
this one has a truly spectacular twist.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.620
From birth, Michael was bathed in&nbsp;
privilege. New York was his playground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:31.500
a world of luxury and abundance. Spending&nbsp;
his days beside future titans of politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:38.160
and industry at some of America’s most acclaimed&nbsp;
academies. He graduated from Harvard in 1960.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.480
The powerful pedigree of Michael's&nbsp;
family propelled his academic ascent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.320
but he wasn’t just some spoiled silver-spooned&nbsp;
scion. He boasted big brains as big as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.700
bankroll. With the world at his feet&nbsp;
and a Rockefeller legacy to uphold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.700
his path seemed set in stone. But&nbsp;
first, Michael yearned for an adventure.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.140
The Rockefellers had always been admirers and&nbsp;
patrons of fine art. Michael's grandmother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.720
founded the famous Museum of Modern art in New&nbsp;
York, and his father, Nelson, had a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.140
penchant for what was then called primitive art -&nbsp;
non-western artwork crafted by indigenous peoples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.140
around the world. In 1954, Nelson founded&nbsp;
a gallery in which to showcase his passion.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.480
No force in the universe rivals&nbsp;
a son's desire to make his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.660
proud. And what better way&nbsp;
for Michael to achieve this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.700
than to curate a collection of global&nbsp;
wonders for his father’s new gallery?

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:45.780
In 1961, Michael Rockefeller departed for a daring&nbsp;
art-gathering expedition to Dutch New Guinea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:51.180
home of the Asmat, an ethnic group almost&nbsp;
unknown to the western world at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.480
Visiting the Asmat was like taking a Time Machine&nbsp;
back to the Stone Age. When Michael turned up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.580
they had no textiles or metals -&nbsp;
they hadn't even invented the wheel.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.920
But whilst they weren’t too clued up&nbsp;
on tech, they were masters of arts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.120
crafts. Even today, Asmat wood carvings&nbsp;
are highly sought after by collectors.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.380
Don't get the wrong impression, though&nbsp;
- the Asmat weren't some quiet tribe of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:22.560
master craftsmen who wouldn't say boo to a New&nbsp;
Guinean goose - when they weren't whittling wood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.960
they were waging war. Either that or&nbsp;
engaging in their favourite pastimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.100
of headhunting, ritual cannibalism, and monopoly.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:38.580
Their way of life was a far cry from the silver&nbsp;
spoons and skyscrapers Michael was used to. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:45.360
he was undeterred - in fact, he threw himself into&nbsp;
this passion project with purpose and panache.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.060
Michael’s diary entries from this time reveal&nbsp;
a man entranced by the strange new world into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:58.140
which he'd stumbled. For six months, he lived&nbsp;
amongst Asmat, studying their way of life and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:03.120
procuring art for his father's museum. He&nbsp;
amassed an almighty collection - you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.080
still see most of it today in the Michael C&nbsp;
Rockefeller wing of The Met in New York...

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:14.160
...Which is all very lovely, but as you already&nbsp;
know, this story doesn't have a happy ending.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.140
Michael was travelling with anthropologist&nbsp;
René Wassing and their two local guides when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:28.200
a wave capsized their boat 3 miles from&nbsp;
shore. With no comms equipment on board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.880
the guides swam to get help while Mike&nbsp;
and René stayed with the stricken vessel.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.560
To begin with, they weren't especially&nbsp;
worried. But they were steadily drifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.880
further from the shore, and after&nbsp;
24 hours with no sign of rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:48.120
Michael's patience wore thin. The guides had&nbsp;
clearly abandoned them, and with each passing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.880
second, the swim back to safety stretched out&nbsp;
before them. He stripped to his boxer shorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.020
tied a couple of empty jerry cans around&nbsp;
his waist and struck out for shore.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.620
The question that's fascinated historians&nbsp;
and amateur sleuths ever since is simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.080
what happened next?

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:14.160
For René Wassing, that question has a brief answer&nbsp;
- he stayed with the boat and was rescued by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:21.000
Dutch a few hours later. Their Papuan guides had&nbsp;
sought help as promised, but they’d had to trudge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.720
through several miles of mudflats, mangroves, and&nbsp;
mosquito-infested forest after swimming ashore.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.540
As for Michael? He was busy turning&nbsp;
himself into a modern-day myth.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:42.000
When word of his disappearance reached the&nbsp;
Rockefellers in the US, they used their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.280
plentiful political and financial leverage to&nbsp;
launch the largest manhunt in the history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.580
the South Pacific, involving thousands of locals&nbsp;
and a multitude of ships, helicopters, and spotter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:59.580
planes. Michael's father and twin sister flew to&nbsp;
Dutch New Guinea to personally oversee the search.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.340
Despite this monumental effort, no&nbsp;
trace of Michael was ever found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:13.560
and after 10 fruitless days, the Rockefellers&nbsp;
returned home. The manhunt was officially called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.160
off 2 weeks later, and Michael was declared&nbsp;
legally dead by drowning in 1964. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:27.480
his estate was worth 660,000 thousand dollars -&nbsp;
about 7 million today. Not bad for a graduate.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:32.700
Ever since his disappearance, there have been&nbsp;
doubts about the official version of events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.800
It's generally assumed that René and Michael were&nbsp;
between 10 and 14 miles offshore when Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.920
abandoned the ship. That's a hell of a swim in&nbsp;
open water, but it certainly isn't impossible.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:50.220
Don’t let Michael’s unassuming appearance fool&nbsp;
you - as a university varsity wrestler and former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:56.580
soldier - he was tough as nails. He was also&nbsp;
a supremely strong swimmer, had rudimentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.800
flotation aids with him, and perhaps most&nbsp;
importantly of all - he was fighting for his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:08.640
People can achieve incredible things when failure&nbsp;
means certain death - just watch Squid Game.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.680
It's intriguing that despite the scale&nbsp;
of the search effort, Michael's body was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:21.060
never found. A drowned man roped to jerry&nbsp;
cans should float, but... nothing surfaced.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.040
The local wildlife is often blamed for&nbsp;
this conundrum - hammerhead sharks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.420
saltwater crocodiles infest these waters.&nbsp;
But shark attacks are practically unheard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.840
of in the area, and while the&nbsp;
colossal crocs pose a threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:40.140
they're notoriously messy eaters - if one did get&nbsp;
him, there would’ve been a few chunks left over.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.040
In the years after Michael fell off&nbsp;
the map, several people attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:50.040
replicate the swim he would have faced in&nbsp;
1961. All of them made it to shore alive.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.400
It's for that reason - and others I'll come to in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.540
a minute - that convinced many that&nbsp;
he was still out there. Among them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.340
was Milt Machlin - the renowned editor&nbsp;
of an adventure magazine called Argosy.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.460
In October 1968, Machlin was visited by an&nbsp;
Australian smuggler who claimed to have new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.680
information about Michael’s disappearance.&nbsp;
Little did he know that his “information”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.560
was about to melt Milt Machlin's mind&nbsp;
(try saying that five times quickly).

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.540
This was 1968, remember - 7 years&nbsp;
since Michael disappeared. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.560
while visiting a remote island off the&nbsp;
east coast of New Guinea 10 weeks prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.480
the smuggler claimed to have seen the&nbsp;
lost Rockefeller with his own two eyes.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.860
Now, Milt Machlin was an ex-military man,&nbsp;
explorer, and master of the moustache. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:45.900
above all else, he was a journalist who&nbsp;
knew how to sniff an exclusive scoop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.220
So he packed up his life in New York&nbsp;
and charted a course for New Guinea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:55.020
However, the first leg of his journey&nbsp;
proved fruitless - he visited the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:00.660
the smuggler had mentioned, only to find&nbsp;
it deserted, with no signs of habitation.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.460
But Machlin hadn't flown 9,000 miles to get TKO’d&nbsp;
when the first hurdle smacked him round the head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:16.020
- especially since he'd noticed a curious trend:&nbsp;
nobody in New Guinea believed Michael Rockefeller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:23.940
had drowned in 1961. By interviewing both Dutch&nbsp;
and indigenous locals - Machlin slowly pieced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:31.260
together the events that led to Michael’s death.&nbsp;
What he learned shocked him to the very core.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:40.020
In 1957, four years before Michael arrived in New&nbsp;
Guinea, a battle between two rival Asmat villages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:46.320
saw the deaths of over 100 people in a single&nbsp;
day. The Dutch colonists were incensed - they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.920
were on a mission to 'civilise' the locals, and&nbsp;
100 murders doesn’t exactly scream “high society”.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:59.100
Keen to bring them to heel, the Dutch dispatched&nbsp;
an armed patrol. One thing led to another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:05.460
and when the dust had settled 5 warriors from the&nbsp;
village of Otsjanep lay dead from Dutch gunfire.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.020
Asmat culture was really into all that&nbsp;
‘an eye for an eye’ stuff - if a rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.220
village killed someone in yous,&nbsp;
you had to kill someone in theirs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:19.320
It wasn't about spite - but a central&nbsp;
tenet of their system of spirituality.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:25.080
So, the five dead warriors from Otsjanep&nbsp;
needed to be avenged to restore balance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:30.600
the world. But retaliation against the Dutch&nbsp;
was basically impossible - their guns were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:35.220
so technologically advanced they might&nbsp;
as well have been magic wands. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:40.140
the Dutch weren't the only people&nbsp;
from the 'white tribe' in New Guinea.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:46.080
After a tired and helpless Michael Rockefeller&nbsp;
washed ashore near Otsjanep, the Asmat saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.360
an opportunity to avenge their fallen&nbsp;
brethren. Michael was killed, butchered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.280
and eaten as payback for crimes&nbsp;
he probably wasn't even aware of.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:04.800
Machlin returned to America in April 1969 after 4&nbsp;
months in New Guinea. He had seen and heard enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.360
to be convinced that Michael Rockefeller&nbsp;
was the victim of a cannibalistic killing.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:15.060
He published his findings in a book called 'The&nbsp;
Search for Michael Rockefeller.' It presented a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:21.540
convincing case, but one thing was missing: hard&nbsp;
evidence. Without it, doubts would always remain.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:27.120
Over the years, the story slowly faded from the&nbsp;
public consciousness - considering how frankly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.200
mental the whole thing is, it's surprising&nbsp;
how few people actually know about it.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:37.920
But the case was reignited in 2014 when author&nbsp;
Carl Hoffman published his research into Michael's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.860
disappearance. Like Machlin, Hoffman travelled&nbsp;
to New Guinea in search of clues. Unlike Machlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:48.060
Hoffman also paid a visit to the&nbsp;
Dutch Colonial Archives in the Hague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.040
where he found a plethora of documents,&nbsp;
showcasing that several Dutch officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:12:00.000
and missionaries had suspected Michael's death at&nbsp;
the hands of the Otsjanep headhunters. Crucially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.740
he also discovered that the Dutch had&nbsp;
deliberately suppressed the truth.

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:12.060
As for why? Michael's disappearance occurred&nbsp;
during a tumultuous period in New Guinea's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:16.020
history. The Dutch were in the process&nbsp;
of relinquishing control to Indonesia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:23.280
and they were keen to show the world what a good&nbsp;
job they'd done 'civilising' the locals. Revealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.800
that cannibals had consumed a member of one of the&nbsp;
world's most powerful families would have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.860
PR disaster. So, they did what all governments do&nbsp;
with embarrassing information - they buried it.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.880
By the way, if you're wondering what the&nbsp;
Rockefellers made of these revelations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.540
the truth is nobody outside the&nbsp;
family really knows. Publicly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:46.620
they've always stuck to the official&nbsp;
version - that Michael drowned.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:52.800
Rumours suggest they hired a private investigator&nbsp;
to visit the Asmat in the late 70s. While he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:59.160
there, he allegedly traded a boat engine for&nbsp;
3 human skulls - those of the only white men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:04.680
the Otsjanep had ever killed. Whether or not&nbsp;
any of them belonged to Michael is unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:08.580
but supposedly the investigator&nbsp;
was paid a quarter of a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:13.440
for smuggling skulls back to America. So,&nbsp;
I'll let you draw your own conclusions.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:19.440
By this point in the video, you may believe it’s&nbsp;
an open-and-shut-case - Michael Rockefeller was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.460
surely killed and eaten by the Asmat.&nbsp;
However, it isn’t quite so simple.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:28.620
Consider this - all the documented&nbsp;
evidence we have so far can be traced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:35.580
back to a single source - the Asmat themselves.&nbsp;
No outsiders witnessed Michael's alleged murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:41.820
and his remains have never been found. Everything&nbsp;
we *think* we know comes from Asmat confessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.740
either made to the Dutch in the 60s&nbsp;
or, more recently, to Carl Hoffman.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:53.460
It’s entirely plausible that the Asmat&nbsp;
took credit for the murder to appear more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:59.160
powerful to western visitors. There is a second&nbsp;
possibility though - that the Asmat came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:05.520
the lie to hide an even more remarkable&nbsp;
truth. That’s right - it’s twist time.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.500
Milt Machlin returned from New Guinea in the&nbsp;
late 60s with hundreds of hours of footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:16.560
documenting his visit. The reels lay untouched in&nbsp;
a safe until 2008, when they were recovered by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.200
of all people, Charlton Heston's son Fraser, who&nbsp;
was making a documentary about Machlin's trip.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:28.920
The reels were a goldmine of information, but it&nbsp;
was when Fraser stumbled upon footage of dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:36.300
Asmat canoes charging down a river that he noticed&nbsp;
something absolutely insane - hidden amongst the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:43.500
Asmat was a single naked white guy, apparently one&nbsp;
of the tribe. Could the Asmat have come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:50.520
the whole 'yeah, we ate that guy' thing to cover&nbsp;
the truth - that Michael had become one of them?

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:56.100
The film's too grainy for any serious&nbsp;
analysis, but you have to admit - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:00.960
man bears more than a passing resemblance&nbsp;
to a slightly older Michael Rockefeller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:04.500
Plus, if it isn't him... who the hell is it?

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:09.600
Far-fetched as that sounds, it's worth&nbsp;
pointing out that almost every contemporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.440
report from the time of Michael's death&nbsp;
mentions how the experience transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.340
him. After his son’s disappearance,&nbsp;
Nelson Rockefeller even admitted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:21.660
a TV interview that Michael had been&nbsp;
his happiest during the expedition.

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:26.460
The idea that the heir to one of&nbsp;
the world's richest families gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:32.100
it all up to live out his days among a&nbsp;
primitive tribe in a distant land is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:38.400
tantalising possibility. Could Michael still&nbsp;
be alive to this day, even in his mid-80s?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:43.380
Whilst implausible, considering he’s&nbsp;
been dwelling in the forests of New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:49.380
Guinea without access to modern medical care&nbsp;
for so long, it’s by no means impossible.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:58.920
So - drowned, eaten by sharks, eaten by&nbsp;
*people,* or a lost millionaire who went native?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:00.600
You tell me.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:04.860
Thanks for watching.

